faq:
  - q: "What happens to my bitcoin when I die?"
    a: "It goes to whoever can sign for it, which may not be who you meant. A will or a named beneficiary settles who is entitled, but it doesn&rsquo;t move bitcoin. Bitcoin held on an exchange or at a brokerage passes like a bank account: through probate, unless you named a beneficiary where the custodian allows one. Bitcoin you hold yourself needs your heir to reach a key, through a company that holds one, a timelock on the chain, or keys and instructions you leave. Without one of those, no one can move it."
  - q: "Can I name a beneficiary for my bitcoin?"
    a: "On some accounts, yes. River, Strike, Onramp and Unchained let you name transfer-on-death beneficiaries, and a brokerage account holding spot bitcoin ETF shares can often carry a transfer-on-death registration. Coinbase, Kraken, Gemini and a Fidelity Crypto taxable account don&rsquo;t offer one, so those pass with your estate. A beneficiary settles who is entitled; for bitcoin you hold yourself, your heir still needs a key."
  - q: "Do heirs pay capital gains tax on inherited bitcoin?"
    a: "Under US federal rules the IRS treats bitcoin as property, and property inherited from someone who has died generally takes its fair market value on the date of death as the heir&rsquo;s basis, so the gain built up in the owner&rsquo;s lifetime isn&rsquo;t taxed when the heir sells. Bitcoin given away during your life keeps your basis instead, and bitcoin inside a traditional IRA is different again: beneficiaries owe income tax on what they withdraw. State rules vary. This summarizes IRS publications; it isn&rsquo;t tax advice."
  - q: "Which bitcoin inheritance service is best?"
    a: "This page doesn&rsquo;t rank them, because each solution trades one dependency for another. Solutions that rely on a company (AnchorWatch, Bitkey, Casa, Nunchuk&rsquo;s off-chain version, Onramp, Swan Vault, Unchained) depend on its claim process, and if the company fails your heirs need enough of your keys to go around it; timelock solutions (Liana, Nunchuk&rsquo;s on-chain version) depend on you renewing on time and your heir handling a key; custodial accounts go through probate unless a beneficiary is named. The page&rsquo;s three questions show what each solution would ask of your heirs."
  - q: "What is a timelock inheritance plan?"
    a: "A wallet whose spending rules change after a set time, enforced by bitcoin itself. Your key can always spend; an heir&rsquo;s key can spend only after the bitcoin has sat unmoved for a period you choose, or after a date you set. You keep it closed by moving the bitcoin before the time runs out. It needs no company, but it can&rsquo;t tell death from a long illness or a forgotten renewal, and your heir needs the key and the wallet&rsquo;s details. Liana and Nunchuk&rsquo;s on-chain version work this way."
