<section class="hero">
    <h1>Bitcoin Inheritance <em>Planning</em></h1>
    <div class="hero-subtitle">Passing on your bitcoin: the solutions compared, and what each asks of your heirs.</div>
    <p class="subtitle">If you died tomorrow, could your family get your bitcoin, and what would they have to do? First, what every plan has to solve. Then three questions about your heirs, and each solution compared, described the same way from the provider&rsquo;s own pages. Nothing here is ranked or recommended.</p>
</section>

<main class="content-area">
    {% include 'components/tool-framing.njk' %}

    {#- What every plan has to solve: the three-dependency spine (ruling 2). The
        table rows come from the groups in inheritance.json, so the spine and the
        card groups can't drift apart. -#}
    <section class="bi-solve" id="what-to-solve" aria-labelledby="solveTitle">
        <div class="bi-eyebrow">Before any provider</div>
        <h2 id="solveTitle" class="bi-h2">What every plan has to solve</h2>
        <p class="bi-lede">Most of us know we will die but don&rsquo;t expect it soon, so the planning gets put off, and with bitcoin, putting it off can cost more than with anything else you own. Bitcoin is the first major asset with no registrar to petition. A court can order a bank or a broker to hand an account to your executor; nothing can make the bitcoin network move bitcoin without a valid signature. So every plan has to settle two things, and it fails if they don&rsquo;t meet.</p>
        <div class="bi-two">
            <div class="bi-two-cell">
                <h3>Who is entitled</h3>
                <p>Legal title: a will, a trust, or a beneficiary named on an account. It decides who should get the bitcoin, but on its own it can&rsquo;t move any.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bi-two-cell">
                <h3>Who can sign</h3>
                <p>Access: a key, or a company that holds one. It moves the bitcoin to whoever is entitled. An heir with title and no key is stuck, and so is a key with no title.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="bi-solve-p">Every solution puts the ability to sign in one of three places: with a company that holds a key, in a timelock written into the wallet itself, or with a custodian that holds the bitcoin. Your heirs then depend on that company, that timelock or that custodian.</p>
        <div class="bi-spine-wrap">
            <table class="bi-spine">
                <thead>
                    <tr><th scope="col">Where the signing sits</th><th scope="col">What your heirs depend on</th><th scope="col">What can go wrong</th><th scope="col">On this page</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                {% for g in inheritance.groups %}
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><a href="#{{ g.id }}">{{ g.title }}</a><span class="bi-spine-how">{{ g.how }}{% if g.howTip %}{{ tip(g.howTip.title, g.howTip.body) }}{% endif %}{{ g.howRest }}</span></th>
                        <td data-label="What your heirs depend on">{{ g.depends }}</td>
                        <td data-label="What can go wrong">{{ g.risk }}</td>
                        <td class="bi-spine-who" data-label="On this page">{% for p in inheritance.plans %}{% if p.group == g.id %}<span class="bi-who"><a href="#card-{{ p.id }}">{{ planName(p) }}</a></span>{% endif %}{% endfor %}</td>
                    </tr>
                {% endfor %}
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="bi-moral">Every solution leaves your heirs depending on something. The choice is what.</p>
        <p class="bi-solve-p">Whichever you choose, your heirs have to know the plan exists: where the instructions are, who to call, and what not to touch. A plan nobody can find works no better than no plan.</p>
