    <section class="bi-prose" id="what-can-go-wrong">
        <h2>What can go wrong, solution by solution</h2>
        <p class="bi-lede">Every solution involves tradeoffs; there is no silver bullet.</p>
        <p><strong>A company&rsquo;s help</strong> puts a company in your heirs&rsquo; path. If the company closes, freezes accounts or changes its terms before they claim, the claim process stops, though in most of these solutions the bitcoin stays where it is, in a wallet the company can&rsquo;t move alone. Most of these solutions keep a way around the company, but it usually needs more of your keys than an heir is given. A common setup: you hold two keys of a 2-of-3 multisig, enough to move the bitcoin yourself, and give your heir one, so your heir can&rsquo;t move anything alone while you are alive; after your death, that one key needs the company&rsquo;s, or another of yours, to make two. Casa&rsquo;s Sovereign Recovery and Unchained&rsquo;s Caravan route need two keys, and Bitkey&rsquo;s exit kit is for the owner, not the heir. The waiting periods are there because anyone named can start a claim while you are alive.</p>
        <p><strong>A timelock</strong> can&rsquo;t tell death from a long stay in hospital or a forgotten renewal. Miss a refresh and your heir&rsquo;s key can spend while you are alive; lose the recovery key or the wallet&rsquo;s description and no one can spend at all. There is nobody to ask for an exception.</p>
        <p><strong>A custodian</strong> puts a company back between you and your bitcoin, which is the arrangement self-custody exists to avoid. If the custodian fails, your heirs inherit a claim on it rather than bitcoin. Without a named beneficiary, they wait for probate before anything moves.</p>
        <p><strong>No plan at all</strong> is, in my judgment, the most common way bitcoin is lost at a death. Estimates of lost bitcoin rest on how long unmoved bitcoin is presumed gone: in 2017 Chainalysis put it at 2.78 to 3.79 million, counting coins unmoved for years and about a million attributed to Satoshi, and the count doesn&rsquo;t say how much was lost to death rather than to lost keys (<a href="https://fortune.com/2017/11/25/lost-bitcoins" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Fortune, reporting Chainalysis</a>). In 2024, Chainalysis counted 1.8 million unmoved since 2014, some of which has since moved again (<a href="https://fortune.com/crypto/2024/04/24/bitcoin-wallets-waking-up-lost-coins-satoshi" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Fortune</a>).</p>
        <p><strong>Scams aimed at families.</strong> The FBI has warned twice about fictitious law firms that contact people who have lost cryptocurrency, claim to work with government agencies, and charge fees to &ldquo;recover&rdquo; it (<a href="https://www.ic3.gov/PSA/2024/PSA240624" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">2024</a>, <a href="https://www.ic3.gov/PSA/2025/PSA250813" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">2025</a>). A grieving family that can&rsquo;t reach a relative&rsquo;s bitcoin is the audience they want. Law enforcement doesn&rsquo;t charge fees to investigate, no law firm is an official partner of a US agency, and no one needs your seed phrase to help with an estate.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="bi-prose" id="do-it-yourself">
        <h2>Doing it yourself, with no product</h2>
        <p>Many people pass bitcoin on with no company at all. The common ways, each with its own cost:</p>
        <ul class="bi-diy">
            <li><strong>A letter of instruction.</strong> A plain document kept with your will: what you hold, where the keys and backups are, which wallet software to use, and who to call. It sets nothing up by itself, and it shouldn&rsquo;t contain a seed phrase, because a will passes through several hands.</li>
            <li><strong>A multisig with family keys.</strong> For example, two of three keys: you hold two, a family member one. At your death your heir needs a second key and the wallet&rsquo;s configuration. It is free and no company is involved; it also has no one to call, and everyone holding a key has to understand it.</li>
            <li><strong>One seed phrase, left to an heir.</strong> The simplest handover, and the most exposed: whoever finds the phrase controls the bitcoin, now, whether or not you have died.</li>
            <li><strong>A timelock wallet you run yourself.</strong> Liana, on its card above, is free software for this.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    {#- The US law panel (ruling 4): sourced facts, no steps, US-flagged, with the
        not-advice and state-law caveats. Counsel-attention tier: change a line
        only with its source re-read (MONTHLY_REFRESH §7.7). -#}
    <section class="bi-law" id="us-law" aria-labelledby="lawTitle">
        <div class="bi-law-head">
            <h2 id="lawTitle" class="bi-law-title">Inheritance and US law: facts that apply to any plan</h2>
            <span class="bi-tag qual">US federal &middot; not tax or legal advice</span>
        </div>
        <p class="bi-law-lede">These hold whichever solution you choose. They are facts from the IRS and the SEC, not steps: state law varies, and an estate attorney, and for taxes a CPA, applies them to a particular estate.</p>
        <dl class="bi-law-grid">
            <div><dt>Bitcoin is property for tax</dt><dd>For US tax purposes, the IRS treats digital assets, bitcoin included, as property, not currency. <a class="bi-src" href="https://www.irs.gov/digitalassets" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">IRS</a></dd></div>
            <div><dt>Inherited property takes a new basis</dt><dd>The basis of property inherited from someone who has died is generally its fair market value on the date of death, whether or not an estate tax return is filed. No IRS page I read applies this to bitcoin by name; it is the rule for property. <a class="bi-src" href="https://www.irs.gov/node/17430" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">IRS</a> <a class="bi-src" href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/26/1.1014-1" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">26 CFR 1.1014-1</a></dd></div>
            <div><dt>A gift keeps the giver&rsquo;s basis</dt><dd>Property received as a gift generally keeps the giver&rsquo;s basis, so bitcoin given away during your life carries the gain built up in your hands. <a class="bi-src" href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p551" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">IRS Pub. 551</a></dd></div>
            <div><dt>Inherited IRAs work differently</dt><dd>Beneficiaries of an IRA must include taxable distributions in their income. From a traditional IRA only the owner&rsquo;s basis from nondeductible contributions carries over, so bitcoin held there gets no date-of-death basis; withdrawals of contributions from an inherited Roth IRA are tax free. <a class="bi-src" href="https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/retirement-topics-beneficiary" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">IRS</a> <a class="bi-src" href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p590b" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">IRS Pub. 590-B</a></dd></div>
            <div><dt>The federal estate tax</dt><dd>For deaths in 2026, the federal basic exclusion amount is $15,000,000 a person, up from $13,990,000 for 2025. State estate and inheritance taxes are separate. <a class="bi-src" href="https://irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/whats-new-estate-and-gift-tax" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">IRS</a></dd></div>
            <div><dt>Transfer on death</dt><dd>A transfer-on-death registration passes securities, such as spot bitcoin ETF shares, to a named person without probate; the beneficiary re-registers them with a death certificate. State law governs it, and each brokerage decides whether to offer it. <a class="bi-src" href="https://www.investor.gov/additional-resources/information/seniors/transferring-assets" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">SEC, Investor.gov</a></dd></div>
            <div><dt>Executors and digital assets</dt><dd>State laws based on the Revised Uniform Fiduciary Access to Digital Assets Act (2015) let an executor or trustee ask a custodian for access to an account. They work through custodians: for bitcoin whose keys no custodian holds, a court order has no one to reach. <a class="bi-src" href="https://alaskabar.org/wp-content/uploads/RUFADAA-ULC_Final_with_Comments.pdf" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Uniform Law Commission</a></dd></div>
            <div><dt>Valuing bitcoin at death</dt><dd>The IRS values virtual currency in US dollars at an exchange rate determined in a reasonable manner that is consistently applied; for an heir, the value on the date of death is the basis. <a class="bi-src" href="https://irs.gov/virtualcurrencyfaqs" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">IRS, Notice 2014-21</a></dd></div>
        </dl>
        <p class="bi-law-foot">Read on 26 November 2026. Not tax or legal advice: a summary of federal publications that apply to estates in general. State law, the kind of account, and the rest of an estate all change the answer for a particular family; an estate attorney applies these to yours.</p>
    </section>

    {#- The dated change log, as on /bitcoin-lenders (lenders v2 C). Rendered from
        src/_data/inheritanceLog.json via inheritanceLogIndex.js. -#}
    <section class="bi-log" id="what-changed" aria-labelledby="biLogTitle">
        <div class="bi-log-head">
            <h2 id="biLogTitle" class="bi-log-title">What changed</h2>
            <p class="bi-log-lede">Providers change their terms, and some add features this page should carry. Every card is re-read against the provider&rsquo;s own pages each month, and every check is logged here, newest first, with whatever it found.{% if inheritanceLogIndex.nextCheckLabel %} Next check: <strong>{{ inheritanceLogIndex.nextCheckLabel }}</strong>.{% endif %}</p>
        </div>
        {% macro logItem(e) -%}
            <li class="bi-log-item bi-log-{{ e.kind }}">
                <time datetime="{{ e.date }}">{{ e.label }}</time>
                <span class="bi-log-who">{% if e.plan %}<a href="#card-{{ e.plan }}">{{ e.name }}</a>{% elif e.kind == 'check' %}Cards checked{% else %}This page{% endif %}</span>
                <span class="bi-log-text">{{ e.text }}{% if e.source %} <a class="bi-log-src" href="{{ e.source }}" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Source <span aria-hidden="true">&nearr;</span></a>{% endif %}</span>
            </li>
        {%- endmacro %}
        <ol class="bi-log-list">
            {% for e in inheritanceLogIndex.entries %}{% if loop.index <= 5 %}{{ logItem(e) }}{% endif %}{% endfor %}
        </ol>
        {% if inheritanceLogIndex.entries.length > 5 %}
        <details class="bi-log-more">
            <summary>Earlier ({{ inheritanceLogIndex.entries.length - 5 }})</summary>
            <ol class="bi-log-list">
                {% for e in inheritanceLogIndex.entries %}{% if loop.index > 5 %}{{ logItem(e) }}{% endif %}{% endfor %}
            </ol>
        </details>
        {% endif %}
    </section>


    <section class="bi-prose" id="what-this-is-not">
        <h2>What this page is, and isn&rsquo;t</h2>
        <p>It is a description of how bitcoin passes to heirs today, as each provider describes its own service, read on the date each card carries. It carries no affiliate or referral links, and no provider has paid for a card. It isn&rsquo;t a recommendation of any solution or provider, and it isn&rsquo;t legal or tax advice: an estate plan is a legal document, and the US law panel says where a lawyer comes in.</p>
        <p><strong>A relationship to declare:</strong> I hold an Unchained IRA vault and pay Unchained&rsquo;s standard fee for it. Unchained&rsquo;s card is written from Unchained&rsquo;s own pages, like every other.</p>
        <p>Figures come from each provider&rsquo;s own pages, never from a competitor&rsquo;s comparison: two comparison tables that providers on this page publish were found to misstate the services they compare. If a card is wrong or a term has moved, the <a href="#pfWrap">feedback form</a> below reaches the person who maintains it.</p>
    </section>
</main>
